nner here i will uh play some music  from my iphone or from my ipod nano but  uh my wife certainly doesn't want to  listen to korean uh while we're eating  uh anytime i'm listening to korean or  russian or ukrainian i put on my  earphones and of course nowadays you  have  um  bluetooth  bluetooth earphones like these that i  bought that are just phenomenal  but if i'm gonna have a bath or  something or if i'm doing exercises here  i ruined my back so i've got to do these  back exercises three times a day  it's just as easy to throw this down  and listen while doing those exercises  so i mean look at the technology we got  the ipod nano we got ipad not to mention  the computer  uh iphone for flashcards this for casual  listening all using bluetooth  technology  um  by the way where did the flash cards  come from the flashcards come from the  words that i have saved which i save on  the computer which are then highlighted  in yellow so that uh you know when i  read them on my  ipad  uh you know i can just touch them and  see what the meaning is and all this  kind of stuff  so  now so with korean that's the routine  now with ukrainian with korean if i  listen to shi satong i understand 10 15  20  with ukrainian i understand depending on  the context 85 percent uh but there's  still words there that i want to learn  so with korean or at least with  ukrainian  i download podcasts from  most  frequently from romance radio and  they're very interesting interviews  there there they don't offer  um  transcripts however i also go to radio  swaboda where they will occasionally  have  an interview with a transcript so i will  download those  but i also import a lot of content  that's as i said before from uh  ukrainian pravda from uh wherever i can  find it or references that i get on my  twitter feed and i read through those  and save words and phrases on the  computer or on the ipad  uh  with ukrainian and russian i can do all  of the linking on the ipad because i'm  not looking to get  you know uh  chinese characters or anything like that  the dictionary works fine on the ipad  so with ukrainian i guess i do a little  more reading than listening but a bit of  both with russian i basically just  listen because 95  i get there's the odd word that i don't  get if i'm really interested uh then i  will import the text from echo must be  where as i've said before you always  have transcript with audio a tremendous  source right now there's some pressure  on ecommerce fee  which is to my mind quite unfair because  you can find a number of different  political positions that echo must be  people who support the government who  oppose the government and it's all their  audio with transcript tremendous  resource for russian learners so that's  how i use all this different stuff  and the way i listen course differs if  i'm listening to the korean where i  understand 10 15 20  i am motivated because i'm trying to  understand more and and and now i  clearly hear the words but they're words  that i don't yet know  or they're words that in fact i do know  but i'm not able yet to recognize them  when i hear them uh whereas i do  understand when i read because  any missing words i can look up uh by  you know touching the screen and so that  the whole context is clearer and as i  continue working this uh shisatong  podcast that i've had transcribed i will  eventually get to where i understand it  when it's spoken by that time my  transcriber will have another podcast  done for me and i continue the process  uh when i listen to ukrainian or russian  i'm essentially listening for the  meaning i'm interested in the events in  ukraine i'm interested hearing different  opinions so  i'm just listening for for or reading  for interest and in that process  gradually improving my skills now i do  dabble in some other languages just if  someone for example on my twitter feed  sends me to an interesting article in  german  and i've done so little work on link  uh 